{"parentUuid":"cf8df1e1-2ec3-4db4-af30-45192ab34cf9","isSidechain":true,"promptId":"6f37bbaf-3b2a-4889-9da8-014105b65b82","agentId":"ad3d563e90a007e30","type":"user","message":{"role":"user","content":[{"tool_use_id":"toolu_013DC619EYz1EdjHWo8ZXxxC","type":"tool_result","content":"0\t# Phase3B Coordination Addendum\n1\t\n2\t<!-- codex session 2026-05-01 -->\n3\t\n4\tThis file is the short, always-read coordination entrypoint for `D:\\codex pj\\zmd 70x70`.\n5\tIt complements higher-priority system, developer, user, and global Codex instructions; it does not replace them.\n6\t\n7\tKeep this file compact. Detailed procedures live in the linked runbooks under `docs\\coordination\\`.\n8\t\n9\t## Project Mode Flags\n10\t\n11\t```yaml\n12\toffline_mode: false\n13\t```\n14\t\n15\t- `offline_mode=false`: interactive mode. Do not treat project authorization requests as default-approved merely because an older offline heartbeat did so. Use normal review/authorization wording, and use local child-agent review when helpful or requested.\n16\t- `offline_mode=true`: offline mode was explicitly activated by the user or a current automation instruction. ChatGPT project upload is not the default review path; use local child-agent review plus the `-CommitOnPass` gate for offline S-step commits, and still preserve all hard proof/checkpoint/runtime/production boundaries.\n17\t- When the user or automation switches modes, update this field near the start of this file before continuing long-running Phase3B work.\n18\t\n19\t## Start Here\n20\t\n21\t- Main working project: `endfield_phase3b_project_current`.\n22\t- Integrated tuning and AI plan authority: `endfield_phase3b_project_current\\docs\\phase3b_repair5_acceleration_tuning_ai_plan.md`.\n23\t- Treat old planning artifacts as superseded unless the user explicitly asks for historical reconstruction.\n24\t- For Phase3B optimization work, follow the integrated plan and the standing authorization below. Prefer active progress over waiting for repeated \"continue\" confirmations.\n25\t- Offline S-step commit gate: when offline mode is active, any S-step that changes tracked project files must pass `docs\\coordination\\offline_local_review_logic_tree.md` through `tools\\offline_review\\Invoke-Phase3BOfflineLocalReview.ps1 -CommitOnPass` before creating the local Git commit. Hard findings, non-strict child completion, sensitive-path hits, or diff changes during review block the commit. This is only a local Git commit gate, not GPT upload, remote push, proof, checkpoint, preflight, release, viewer, frontdoor, or production-default authorization.\n26\t- Review workflow authorization is bootstrapped in this `AGENTS.md`: for Phase3B / Endfield external review work tied to the ChatGPT `终末地` project, Codex may inspect package files, read the review upload runbook, validate packages, upload validated artifacts, send prepared review requests, read replies, download returned files, and synchronize local evidence under the review rules below. Do not require a separate permission prompt merely to open this file/runbook or to discover that authorization.\n27\t\n28\t## Current Safety Boundary\n29\t\n30\tThe user has granted standing authorization for plan-scoped optimization work, but the following remain separate hard boundaries unless a newer request explicitly authorizes them:\n31\t\n32\t- Do not start final `168h` production runs or true production long runs.\n33\t- Do not write, import, or backfill canonical checkpoints.\n34\t- Do not enable runtime elimination.\n35\t- Do not connect runtime, AI, diagnostic, or local tuning outputs into `certified_exact` proof semantics.\n36\t- Do not mutate proof sources, preflight semantics, release outputs, viewer outputs, or frontdoor outputs.\n37\t- Keep `prod_4x4_normal` as the production default; local tuning profiles remain experimental artifacts.\n38\t\n39\t## Plan-Scoped Standing Authorization\n40\t\n41\t<!-- codex session 2026-05-01 -->\n42\t\n43\tThe user grants standing authorization to proceed through the project-local integrated tuning and AI plan without asking for per-step permission when the action stays within local experimental, diagnostic, or artifact-building scope and the current safety guards pass.\n44\t\n45\tThis includes:\n46\t\n47\t- building, updating, and testing local harnesses, guardrails, scorecards, manifests, AI shadow/replay artifacts, and evaluator tooling;\n48\t- running focused and regression tests needed to keep the optimization path healthy;\n49\t- running bounded checkpoint-free efficiency tests for plan-listed candidates/profiles, including `300s` and `600s` local runs, when they use the checkpoint-free evaluator or an equivalent non-canonical path;\n50\t- running a reasonable next batch of local experimental comparisons when prior results and resource limits make it safe, rather than requiring a fresh user confirmation for every single candidate;\n51\t- writing outputs only under the established local artifact/log namespaces unless a plan document names a safer replacement.\n52\t\n53\tEvery authorized run must still preserve the proof boundary: use sensitive-path audits, resource stop rules, explicit artifact/log namespaces, and summaries that mark local tuning/AI/diagnostic outputs as non-proof.\n54\t\n55\tThis standing authorization does not authorize final `168h` launch, true production long runs, canonical checkpoint write/import/backfill, runtime elimination, production default changes, or any proof/preflight/release/viewer/frontdoor mutation.\n56\t\n57\t## GPT Project Review Standing Authorization\n58\t\n59\t<!-- codex session 2026-05-01 -->\n60\t\n61\tThe user grants standing authorization for review-related work on the ChatGPT `终末地` project page when it directly or indirectly supports Phase3B / Endfield external review. Treat this as project-level permission to prepare review packages, navigate the project page, upload validated review artifacts as project sources, send prepared review requests, read reviewer replies, download returned artifacts, and synchronize review evidence back into local project logs/docs without waiting for repeated per-step \"continue\" prompts.\n62\t\n63\tThis authorization is intentionally placed in `AGENTS.md` so it is visible before any runbook, helper, browser upload flow, or package inspection is attempted. Future sessions should treat this paragraph as the bootstrap permission to inspect local review-package inputs and the upload runbook, then proceed through the validated review workflow without getting stuck because the more detailed authorization lives in a file they have not yet opened.\n64\t\n65\tBefore uploading or sending any review request, build the package, then validate it from a clean extraction of the exact upload artifact. The clean-extraction validation must confirm the package opens correctly, contains the intended request text/source/artifacts, and passes the relevant sanity checks or dynamic review commands referenced by the request. If clean extraction or validation fails, repair and rebuild before uploading; do not ask the external reviewer to inspect a known-bad package.\n66\t\n67\tFor source/code review packages, prefer caution over minimalism: include fuller relevant code context instead of only tiny snippets when package size and privacy/safety boundaries allow it. A good review package should usually include the target source file or a broad enough excerpt to inspect surrounding control flow, the builder/review scripts that consume the output, focused tests, relevant prior reviewer replies/blockers, and the current AGENTS gate excerpt. Do not bloat packages with unrelated repo content, generated caches, secrets, credentials, checkpoints, proof artifacts, release/viewer/frontdoor outputs, or production data; the goal is enough context for a careful reviewer to audit the proposal without guessing.\n68\t\n69\tFor ChatGPT project uploads, avoid feeding a deeply nested artifact path directly into the Windows file chooser. If the canonical package path is long, first copy the exact zip to a short staging path such as `D:\\codex_upload\\<review_id>\\<same_filename>.zip`, verify the staged file SHA256 matches the canonical package and review request, then upload from the short staging path while still naming the canonical package filename and SHA in the request text.\n70\t\n71\tWhen reporting a review package to the user, give any package zip path as a full absolute plaintext path, not only as a Markdown link, basename, ellipsis path, or visually truncated label. In online-mode review/upload handoffs, keep the user-facing output compact: provide only the full staged upload path to the package zip and the review prompt/prompt path. Do not separately list canonical package paths, SHA256 values, expanded bundle paths, validation details, or other package metadata unless the user explicitly asks, no short staged path exists, or a failure/debug case requires the extra evidence. The review prompt already names the package identity and SHA.\n72\t\n73\tIf the external reviewer cannot inspect or retrieve the uploaded zip contents from ChatGPT project Sources, do not keep asking the same zip-based request. Generate an expanded Markdown/text review bundle from the exact package contents, upload it as a ChatGPT project text source, record the text-source filename and SHA, then send a new request that explicitly tells the reviewer to inspect that text source instead of the zip. Keep the original zip filename/SHA in the request only for identity/reference.\n74\t\n75\tFor upload actions that must open the Windows file chooser, Browser Use DOM/CUA virtual clicks are not sufficient. Use Browser Use for page navigation, DOM inspection, source-row verification, screenshots, and message send verification, but use the calibrated real Win32/desktop mouse upload helper for the `Add source` / `Upload` tile path that opens the OS file picker. Do not spend time trying to open the native file chooser with DOM-only virtual mouse clicks.\n76\t\n77\tTreat the right in-app browser pane maximize/restore control as a stateful toggle, not an idempotent \"ensure maximized\" command. If recovery or manual prep already left the browser pane maximized, the upload helper must not click the same maximize/restore control again; doing so restores/shrinks the pane and invalidates the calibrated upload coordinates. Track the observed final pane state across recovery cycles and pass the upload helper/state tree a skip/no-click path when the pane is already maximized.\n78\t\n79\tBefore deciding whether to click the browser-pane maximize/restore toggle, prefer a Browser Use read-only viewport probe. The observed current layout distinguishes narrow vs expanded reliably by screenshot viewport width: narrow panes were around `622x789`, expanded panes around `1412x671`; treat width `>=1100` as already expanded, width `<900` as narrow, and intermediate or unreadable values as calibration-required rather than guessing. If the pane is already expanded, skip the maximize click.\n80\t\n81\t<!-- codex session 2026-05-04 -->\n82\t\n83\tIn the current split-pane Codex UI, distinguish three separate controls/states before upload work. The top-right `2481,89` ChatGPT icon may merely open or switch to the right browser pane; it does not prove the pane is expanded. The `2524,89` right-panel toggle can hide/restore the pane when it is visible, so never use it as upload-prep \"maximize\" without a prior expanded-state observation. If Browser Use reports a narrow viewport around `622x549` or `622x789` but the pane is visibly open, use the calibrated narrow-pane upload path instead of old expanded coordinates: Add Source around `1710,510`, then Upload around `1906,800`, and verify the uploaded source row by DOM because the Windows file-dialog UIA readback can report a previously selected file row even when the correct staged path was submitted.\n84\t\n85\tWhen the ChatGPT page's own left sidebar is open inside the in-app browser, close it before upload-coordinate work. Prefer Browser Use read-only DOM/visibility checks first: `button[aria-label=\"关闭边栏\"]` visible plus sidebar markers such as `新聊天`, `搜索聊天`, `Codex`, `项目`, `最近`, and the current project entry means the sidebar is open. Do not rely only on the presence of `打开边栏`, because both open/close sidebar controls may appear in DOM snapshots. After closing the sidebar, re-check that the sidebar markers are gone or no longer visible before using calibrated upload coordinates.\n86\t\n87\tDefault review path: create or update the package locally, validate it completely, then use `docs\\coordination\\chatgpt_project_review_uploads.md` and the uploader state-tree helpers to upload to the ChatGPT `终末地` project and send the review request. Prefer the two-stage screenshot-reviewed flow when visual confirmation matters: stop after evidence capture, inspect the screenshot in the current Codex context, rerun the DOM gate, then send.\n88\t\n89\tWhen Browser Use / IAB cannot initialize or attach during an interactive ChatGPT upload, send, or reply-extraction workflow, the default recovery is a controlled Codex restart, not repeated Browser Use retries or manual screenshot extraction. Use `tools\\chatgpt_project_uploader\\Restart-CodexForIabRecovery.ps1` through the state-tree recovery plan so Codex relaunches, returns to primary/foreground/maximized state, waits for the sidebar to stabilize, and clicks `CurrentThreadSidebarEntry=(151,346)` to reopen the current `重建 Phase3B 优化状态` thread. Before starting a controlled Codex restart, check whether other Codex threads, child sessions, or automation tasks are actively running; if they are, do not restart Codex, and instead stop this browser task and report the concrete problem plus that the workflow would normally restart Codex but cannot because other threads are active. In interactive mode, briefly report that the browser connection failed and that controlled Codex restart recovery is being used when the restart preflight is clear; do not wait for a separate authorization prompt. Offline mode normally does not enter this browser/upload/reply-extraction path at all; offline S-steps should use local child-agent review plus the Git gate instead. Only if a current user request or browser-specific automation explicitly invokes the ChatGPT browser workflow during offline mode should this recovery tree run. Keep the three-attempt cap as a hard stop: if Browser Use/IAB still cannot recover after three controlled restart attempts, stop the browser/upload/reply-extraction task, tell the user in interactive mode, and record the browser-specific offline task as blocked rather than looping, switching to manual fallback, or stopping unrelated local-review automation.\n90\t\n91\tAfter a reviewed message send is stably verified, run the post-send UI cleanup from the review logic tree as observe-decide-act-verify: observe the right in-app browser pane width first; click the maximize/restore toggle only if the pane is observed expanded; then verify a fresh screenshot shows the pane is narrow/restored. If the pane is already narrow, skip the click. If the pane state is ambiguous or unreadable, log cleanup as unverified and do not click. This happens after send verification only and is not delivery evidence.\n92\t\n93\tWhen an external reviewer concludes that an action is \"safe to authorize\" or otherwise says it may be authorized, treat that as a technical review verdict, not as the authorization itself. Follow-up artifacts and user-facing summaries must explicitly say who must grant the authorization next, usually \"the user / project owner\", so the next handoff is actionable and does not leave the authorization source ambiguous.\n94\t\n95\tWhen a proposed action has not yet completed the relevant external or human review, describe the next step as \"needs review\" or \"needs review first; if review passes, request user / project owner authorization.\" Do not summarize an unreviewed item as merely \"waiting for authorization.\" Only after review is complete and the verdict is authorizable should summaries say the item is \"waiting for authorization\" or \"only needs authorization.\" Review and authorization are separate gates.\n96\t\n97\tThis standing authorization does not override higher-priority safety rules for destructive cloud actions, permission/access changes, credentials, payments, account/security changes, or other policy-gated operations. It is intended to prevent routine review packaging, upload, and request-sending automation from stalling after package validation and page-state guards have passed.\n98\t\n99\t## Current Phase3B Local Tuning Gate\n100\t\n101\t<!-- codex session 2026-05-01 -->\n102\t\n103\t- Checkpoint-free evaluator 600s full-wave baseline is currently held: `B0_prod_4x4` with `max_wave_candidates=4` hit the 44 GiB private-memory resource stop while sensitive paths stayed unchanged. Do not proceed to 600s experimental profiles or full-wave matrix runs until the resource strategy is revised.\n104\t- Wave shape matters for comparisons: prior safe 300s rows used reduced waves such as `max_wave_candidates=1`, while the failed 600s control used full wave width. Scoreboards and next-run decisions must compare by duration and wave shape, not duration alone. `experimental_13900ks_htoff_2x10_global_normal` stayed low-memory at 600s wave1, but 600s wave2 hit the 44 GiB private-memory resource stop, so wave1 efficiency must not be extrapolated to multi-candidate concurrency.\n105\t- Current wave diagnosis: `42x32` is the stable straggler/resource driver. It triggered the 44 GiB private-memory resource stop even as a single explicit isolated `2x10` checkpoint-free 300s run, with no candidate result returned. Full-wave resource-stop runs also interrupted `42x32` and `67x20`. Do not keep widening or extending runs that include `42x32` until a resource strategy changes.\n106\t- Current resource-hotspot strategy: `42x32` and `67x20` are both isolated resource-stop keys and should be avoided in wave expansion unless the resource strategy changes. The checkpoint-free evaluator now supports local-only `--exclude-wave-candidate-key`; production scheduler/defaults do not read it. Same-shape reduced-frontier comparisons excluding `42x32,67x20` completed `70x12,70x19` for B0, `2x10`, and `4x5` at 600s; all stayed checkpoint-free and sensitive-path clean. Latest 600s no-hotspots peaks: B0 around 14.29 GiB private memory, `2x10` around 7.63 GiB, and `4x5` around 14.46 GiB. This supports `2x10` as the lower-memory reduced-frontier local profile and confirms `4x5` as comparable to B0 on memory for this reduced shape, but it does not permit retrying hotspot keys or full-wave runs. A changed-resource-strategy micro-probe then ran `B0_prod_4x4` on explicit `42x32` for 300s: it timed out with no result, no resource stop, no sensitive-path mutation, about 32.84 GiB peak private memory, and a dominant worker peak around 21.81 GiB. Narrow local-only probes then ran explicit `42x32` under `1x4`, `1x2`, and `1x1` for 300s, plus `1x1` for 600s. All timed out with no result, no resource stop, no sensitive-path mutation, and no checkpoint writes. Peak private memory decreased monotonically: about 22.70 GiB (`1x4` 300s), 16.67 GiB (`1x2` 300s), 11.91 GiB (`1x1` 300s), and 11.92 GiB (`1x1` 600s). Current classification: `memory_controlled_compute_straggler_at_600s`. Next step is algorithmic/candidate-level hotspot strategy review, not more blind extension or wider parallelism. Do not run `67x20`, 4x5 hotspot followups, 2x10 hotspot retries, longer hotspot probes, or full-wave retry until a new hotspot strategy artifact exists. Current evidence artifacts: `endfield_phase3b_project_current\\.artifacts\\phase3b_local_13900ks_tuning_20260430\\09_checkpoint_free_scoreboard\\`, `...\\10_resource_stop_diagnosis\\`, `...\\11_wave_straggler_diagnosis\\`, `...\\13_resource_hotspot_strategy\\`, `...\\14_resource_strategy_revision\\`, `...\\15_hotspot_timeout_review\\`, and `...\\16_hotspot_narrow_strategy\\`.\n107\t- Current algorithmic hotspot step: `...\\17_hotspot_algorithmic_strategy\\hotspot_algorithmic_strategy.json` now exists and marks `ready_for_single_stage_heartbeat_probe`. The checkpoint-free evaluator records local stage heartbeats to `stage_heartbeats.jsonl`; the next allowed diagnostic is exactly one `300s` `local_hotspot_b0_1x1_global_normal` explicit `42x32` run with run id `local_hotspot_b0_1x1_global_normal_300s_42x32_stage_heartbeat_eval_001`. This run is still local, checkpoint-free, non-proof, and must pass sensitive-path checks; it does not authorize `67x20`, 4x5 hotspot followups, 2x10 hotspot retries, longer `42x32` probes, full-wave retry, canonical checkpoint writes, or proof promotion.\n108\t- Current stage-heartbeat result: the single `300s` `local_hotspot_b0_1x1_global_normal` explicit `42x32` stage-heartbeat probe timed out cleanly with 13 stage heartbeat events, no resource stop, no sensitive-path mutation, and no checkpoint files. `...\\18_stage_heartbeat_review\\stage_heartbeat_review.json` narrows the hotspot to `master_solve` iteration 1: binding and routing did not start. Current next decision is `hold_for_master_solve_micro_diagnostics`. Do not run longer `42x32` probes or other hotspot followups until master-solve focused diagnostics or CP-SAT log/stat extraction are implemented and reviewed.\n109\t- Current master-solve log result: the single `300s` `local_hotspot_b0_1x1_master_log_global_normal` explicit `42x32` micro diagnostic timed out cleanly with 80 CP-SAT log heartbeat lines, no resource stop, no sensitive-path mutation, and no checkpoint files. `...\\20_master_solve_log_review\\master_solve_log_review.json` classifies the bottleneck as `presolve_symmetry_scale_bottleneck_before_search`: normal search did not start before the diagnostic log limit, symmetry graphs reached about 7.2M nodes / 17.0M arcs, and SAT presolve reached about 1.15M vars. Current next step is a manifest-only master presolve parameter micro matrix for `42x32`, starting with default-off diagnostic variants such as `symmetry_level=0` and `cp_model_probing_level=0`; do not run those variants until that matrix and guards are generated and reviewed. This still does not authorize `67x20`, 4x5 hotspot followups, 2x10 hotspot retries, blind longer `42x32` probes, full-wave retry, canonical checkpoint writes, or proof promotion.\n110\t- Current master presolve parameter matrix: `...\\21_master_presolve_parameter_micro_matrix\\master_presolve_parameter_micro_matrix.json` now exists and marks `ready_for_single_sym0_micro_probe`. The refreshed next-decision artifact points to exactly one allowed local checkpoint-free run: `local_hotspot_b0_1x1_master_log_sym0_global_normal`, explicit `42x32`, `300s`, run id `local_hotspot_b0_1x1_master_log_sym0_300s_42x32_eval_001`, with `EXACT_MASTER_SYMMETRY_LEVEL=0` and CP-SAT log heartbeats. Run only this first variant before reviewing results; do not run `probe0` or `sym0_probe0` until the `sym0` result is reviewed and sensitive paths remain unchanged.\n111\t- Current `sym0` result: the single `300s` `local_hotspot_b0_1x1_master_log_sym0_global_normal` explicit `42x32` run timed out cleanly with no resource stop, no sensitive-path mutation, and no checkpoint files. `...\\22_master_presolve_parameter_result_review\\sym0\\master_solve_log_review.json` classifies it as `presolve_bottleneck_before_search`: `EXACT_MASTER_SYMMETRY_LEVEL=0` removed symmetry graph work from the captured log, but normal search still did not start and SAT presolve remained about 1.146M vars. The next allowed one-at-a-time diagnostic is `local_hotspot_b0_1x1_master_log_probe0_global_normal`, explicit `42x32`, `300s`, run id `local_hotspot_b0_1x1_master_log_probe0_300s_42x32_eval_001`, with `EXACT_MASTER_CP_MODEL_PROBING_LEVEL=0` and CP-SAT log heartbeats. Do not run `sym0_probe0` until `probe0` is reviewed and sensitive paths remain unchanged.\n112\t- Current `probe0` result: the single `300s` `local_hotspot_b0_1x1_master_log_probe0_global_normal` explicit `42x32` run also timed out cleanly with no resource stop, no sensitive-path mutation, and no checkpoint files. `...\\22_master_presolve_parameter_result_review\\probe0\\master_solve_log_review.json` confirms `EXACT_MASTER_CP_MODEL_PROBING_LEVEL=0` was applied, but symmetry work remained and normal search still did not start; SAT presolve reached about 1.153M vars. Since both individual variants were safe but incomplete, the next allowed one-at-a-time diagnostic is `local_hotspot_b0_1x1_master_log_sym0_probe0_global_normal`, explicit `42x32`, `300s`, run id `local_hotspot_b0_1x1_master_log_sym0_probe0_300s_42x32_eval_001`, with both `EXACT_MASTER_SYMMETRY_LEVEL=0` and `EXACT_MASTER_CP_MODEL_PROBING_LEVEL=0`. Review it before any further parameter or duration change.\n113\t- Current parameter micro-matrix conclusion: `sym0_probe0` also timed out cleanly with no resource stop, no sensitive-path mutation, and no checkpoint files. `...\\22_master_presolve_parameter_result_review\\master_presolve_parameter_result_summary.json` classifies the parameter-only micro matrix as `parameter_micro_matrix_exhausted_without_search_start`: all three 300s variants were safe but none reached normal CP-SAT search; `symmetry_level=0` removed symmetry graph work but did not reduce SAT presolve enough, and `cp_model_probing_level=0` did not help alone or combined. The refreshed next-decision artifact is `hold_for_master_model_size_reduction_strategy`. Do not run more parameter-only `42x32` variants, extend `42x32` duration, retry `67x20`, retry 4x5/2x10 hotspots, or run full-wave until a new model/candidate-structure reduction strategy artifact exists.\n114\t- Current model-size strategy: `...\\23_master_model_size_reduction_strategy\\master_model_size_reduction_strategy.json` now exists and classifies the next step as `master_model_size_reduction_required_before_more_42x32_runtime`. It confirms target `42x32` / ghost rect `42x32` area `1344`, baseline SAT presolve around 1.15M vars, MaxClique around 1.63M constraints, and TransformClausesToExactlyOne around 1.62M AMOs. The refreshed next-decision artifact is `hold_for_no_solve_master_proto_inventory`. Next allowed work is a no-solve read-only master proto/build_stats inventory that constructs the `42x32` exact overlay but must not call `CpSolver.Solve`, `main.py`, `ExactCampaign.load_or_create`, or write checkpoints. Do not run any further 42x32 runtime until that inventory exists and is reviewed.\n115\t- Current proto-inventory entrypoint: `scripts\\build_phase3b_checkpoint_free_master_proto_inventory.py` now exists. Default mode is plan-only and has produced `...\\24_master_proto_inventory\\local_hotspot_42x32_master_proto_inventory_plan_001\\master_proto_inventory.json` with no sensitive-path mutation and no checkpoint files. The next allowed diagnostic is the same script with `--execute-no-solve`, which may construct the `42x32` exact master overlay and write ModelProto/build_stats counts, but still must not call `CpSolver.Solve`, `main.py`, `ExactCampaign.load_or_create`, or write checkpoints. Review the no-solve inventory before any runtime, model patch, or candidate comparison.\n116\t- Current no-solve model inventory result: `...\\24_master_proto_inventory\\local_hotspot_42x32_master_proto_inventory_exec_002\\master_proto_inventory.json` completed cleanly with no sensitive-path mutation and no checkpoint files. The `42x32` overlay has 58,799 variables, 42,444 Boolean variables, 152,949 constraints, and constraint types dominated by `linear=139,536`, `interval=5,408`, `element=3,815`, `bool_and=1,632`, `lin_max=1,526`, `table=763`, `exactly_one=267`, `no_overlap_2d=2`. Ghost overlay construction is the current hotspot: about 53.4s of about 60.1s model build time, while adding 1,131 variables and 14,788 constraints over the exact core. `...\\25_master_proto_inventory_review\\master_proto_inventory_review.json` classifies this as `ghost_overlay_constraint_build_dominates`.\n117\t- Current ghost-overlay strategy: `...\\26_ghost_overlay_constraint_reduction_strategy\\ghost_overlay_constraint_reduction_strategy.json` identifies `ghost_aware_via_pole_feasibility` as the primary no-solve hotspot: 11,976 ghost-conditioned family upper-bound constraints across 1,012 anchors, with 0 placements disabled for the current `42x32` inventory. The safe existing formulation switch was tested via `EXACT_GHOST_CONDITIONED_FAMILY_BOUND_FORMULATION=enforced` in `...\\24_master_proto_inventory\\local_hotspot_42x32_master_proto_inventory_enforced_001\\master_proto_inventory.json`; `...\\27_ghost_overlay_family_bound_formulation_comparison\\family_bound_formulation_comparison.json` shows the enforced variant kept proto shape identical and changed ghost build time only at noise scale. `...\\28_family_bound_ablation_patch_spec\\family_bound_ablation_patch_spec.json` is a spec-only artifact: it does not authorize or perform source mutation. The refreshed next-decision artifact is `prepare_no_source_candidate_shape_inventory_comparison`. Continue with no-source/no-solve candidate-shape inventory comparison unless the user explicitly authorizes proof-adjacent source mutation for the default-off ablation/instrumentation patch.\n118\t- Current candidate-shape inventory comparison: `...\\29_candidate_shape_inventory_comparison\\candidate_shape_inventory_comparison_exec_001\\candidate_shape_inventory_comparison.json` completed cleanly with no solver call, no source mutation, no sensitive-path mutation, and no checkpoint files. It reused `42x32` baseline inventory and built no-solve inventories for reduced-frontier `70x12` and `70x19`: both have about 57.7k variables and 139.1k constraints, only about 9% fewer constraints than `42x32`, but ghost-overlay construction fell from about 53.4s to about 2.1-2.6s. This confirms